      <div class="col-lg-7">
        <span class="section-label">Why This Matters</span>
        <h2 class="mt-2 mb-4">The Alarm on Top of the Fridge</h2>
        <p>A few winters back we were rewiring a power point at an older gentleman's place in Shirley. On the way out, one of our guys spotted a smoke alarm sitting on top of the fridge — battery beside it, a little halo of dust marking the spot on the hallway ceiling where it used to live. It had started chirping one night in 2019, so it came down. It never went back up.</p>
        <p>Here's the thing: nobody in that house was careless. The chirp is annoying, the ladder was in the garage, and life moved on. We see the same story every single week across Christchurch — alarms in drawers, alarms with the battery flat since who knows when, alarms from the 90s that yellowed like old newspaper and quietly stopped doing their job years ago.</p>
        <div class="story-quote">
          House fires don't announce themselves. Most fatal ones happen at night, and it's the smoke — not the flames — that gets people. A working alarm buys you the two or three minutes that make all the difference.
        </div>
        <p>That fridge alarm is basically why we do this work. We fitted three long-life photoelectric alarms in his place before we packed up the van — twenty minutes, job done, and there's no battery to chirp at 3am because it's sealed inside for ten years. If any of this sounds like your hallway ceiling, we should talk.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

